## DESCRIPTION
## Find the projection of a vector onto a plane in R^3.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Inner products)
## DBsection(Projection and distance)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)
## Level(1)
## KEYWORDS('projection', 'plane')

# updated 2022.12.12

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserVectorUtils.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Vector');
Context()->noreduce('(-x)-y', '(-x)+y');

# Define all variables used in the problem statement and solution

do {
    $a = random(1, 4);
    $b = random(1, 4);
    $c = random(1, 4);
    $u = ColumnVector(non_zero_point3D(-3, 3));
    $n = ColumnVector([ $a, $b, $c ]);
} until (gcd($a, $b, $c) == 1) && $u . $n != 0;

$plane = Formula("$a x + $b y + $c z")->reduce;

$v    = ColumnVector(($n . $u) / ($u . $u) * $u);
$proj = $u - $v;

BEGIN_PGML

Find the projection of the vector
[``\vec{u} = [$u] ``] onto the plane [` H: [$plane]=0`].

[` \text{Proj}_H (\vec{u}) `] = [____]{$proj}
Write your answer as a vector, like "<1,2,3>"

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
A way to do this is to project the given vector [`\vec{u}`] onto the normal
to the plane or
[```\text{Proj}_{\vec{n}}(\vec{u}) = \frac{\vec{n}\cdot \vec{u}}
{\vec{u} \cdot \vec{u}} \vec{u}```]
where [`\vec{n} = [$n]`], the normal vector to the plane.
[```\vec{v} = \text{Proj}_{\vec{n}}(\vec{u}) = \frac{[@ $n . $u @]}
{[@ $u . $u @]} \vec{u} = [$v]```]

Lastly, the projection onto the plane will be the original vector [`\vec{u}`]
minus this vector or
[```\text{Proj}_H (\vec{u}) = \vec{u} - \vec{v} = [$proj] ```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();    # This should be the last executable line in the problem.
